index 0000000..15259f6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/drivers/cogent/native/patches/mouseevent/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@
+# Current native mouse-event position
+
+Stage 74 adds the optional `desktop.NativeMouseEventPositioner` attachment
+contract. Button and scroll callbacks prefer its accepted device-pixel position;
+without support, or when it returns false, the original GLFW hardware-cursor
+path remains. General cursor queries, file drops and GLFW-provided move/drag
+coordinates retain their prior paths. A supplied point is neither cached,
+clamped nor scaled twice.
+
+On Cocoa the accessibility/native attachment uses only the current mouse event
+for its exact live window, on the main thread. It converts content-view bounds
+and flipping to top-left logical coordinates and applies the existing desktop
+DPR conversion once. Key/application events, missing/foreign windows or views,
+non-finite coordinates and off-main calls refuse without writing outputs.
+Closing the native attachment withdraws its window reference first. This hook
+posts no work, acquires no render/owner locks and never calls RunOnMain.
+
+The patch/input/output hashes are owned by `native/dependencies.json` and
+`prepare.py`; do not hand-edit generated upstream sources. Original upstream
+files retain their BSD-style licensing; the new test is MIT under this SDK.
+
+## Checks
+
+From `drivers/cogent` after preparation:
+
+```sh
+go -C build/upstream test -race ./system/driver/desktop
+go test -race -tags offscreen -run 'TestNativeMousePosition|TestReviewFieldSettlement' .
+xcrun clang -fobjc-arc -framework Cocoa -Wall -Wextra native/mouse-position-checks.m -o build/mouse-position-checks
+./build/mouse-position-checks
+```
+
+The Cocoa check creates invisible window/view objects and protocol event
+fixtures. It posts or dispatches no input. Its 29 assertions cover all admitted
+mouse event kinds, flipped/offset view bounds, outside-view coordinates,
+window/type/thread/finite/null refusal and unchanged output sentinels. The
+worker uses asynchronous dispatch: synchronous dispatch may execute inline on
+the calling thread and cannot establish an off-main refusal test.
+
+Go tests establish native-point precedence without hardware access, fresh and
+unclamped device pixels, independent window attachments, unavailable-hook
+fallback admission and absent live-window refusal. These are unit/protocol
+proofs. Actual native button/field/scroll/menu/drag interactions and physical
+DPR/cross-display/lifecycle qualification remain separate gates. There is no
+claim of full platform, screen-reader, distribution or visible-latency
+qualification from these checks.
